          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 14 Energy Conversion and Metabolic Compartmentation: Mitochondria and Chloroplasts"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.421,
            0.505,
            0.946
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE MITOCHONDRION 813  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Mitochondrion Has an Outer Membrane and an Inner Membrane 814  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fission, Fusion, Distribution, and Degradation of Mitochondria 815  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Inner Membrane Cristae Contain the Machinery for Electron Transport and ATP Synthesis 817  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Citric Acid Cycle in the Matrix Produces NADH 817  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mitochondria Have Many Essential Roles in Cellular Metabolism 818  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Chemiosmotic Process Couples Oxidation Energy to ATP Production 821  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Energy Derived from Oxidation Is Stored as an Electrochemical Gradient 822  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 823  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE PROTON PUMPS OF THE ELECTRON-TRANSPORT CHAIN 823  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Redox Potential Is a Measure of Electron Affinities 823  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Electron Transfers Release Large Amounts of Energy 824  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Transition Metal Ions and Quinones Accept and Release Electrons Readily 824  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "NADH Transfers Its Electrons to Oxygen Through Three Large Enzyme Complexes Embedded in the Inner Membrane 827  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The NADH Dehydrogenase Complex Contains Separate Modules for Electron Transport and Proton Pumping 828  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cytochrome c Reductase Takes Up and Releases Protons on Opposite Sides of the Crista Membrane, Thereby Pumping Protons 829  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Cytochrome c Oxidase Complex Pumps Protons and Reduces "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{O}_2"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Using a Catalytic Iron-Copper Center 831  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Succinate Dehydrogenase Acts in Both the Electron-Transport Chain and the Citric Acid Cycle 832  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Respiratory Chain Forms a Supercomplex in the Crista Membrane 833  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Protons Can Move Rapidly Through Proteins Along Predefined Pathways 834  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 835  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "ATP PRODUCTION IN MITOCHONDRIA 835  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Large Negative Value of "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\Delta G"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " for ATP Hydrolysis Makes ATP Useful to the Cell 835  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The ATP Synthase Is a Nanomachine That Produces ATP by Rotary Catalysis 837  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Proton-driven Turbines Are Ancient and Critical for Energy Conversion 839  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mitochondrial Cristae Help to Make ATP Synthesis Efficient 840  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Special Transport Proteins Move Solutes Through the Inner Membrane 841  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chemiosmotic Mechanisms First Arose in Bacteria 842  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 842"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "index",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.525,
            0.098,
            0.923,
            0.95
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "CHLOROPLASTS AND PHOTOSYNTHESIS 843  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chloroplasts Resemble Mitochondria but Have a Separate Thylakoid Compartment 843  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chloroplasts Capture Energy from Sunlight and Use It to Fix Carbon 844  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Carbon Fixation Uses ATP and NADPH to Convert "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{CO}_{2}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " into Sugars 845  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Carbon Fixation in Some Plants Is Compartmentalized to Facilitate Growth at Low "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "\\mathrm{CO}_{2}"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Concentrations 846  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Sugars Generated by Carbon Fixation Can Be Stored as Starch or Consumed to Produce ATP 849  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Thylakoid Membranes of Chloroplasts Contain the Protein Complexes Required for Photosynthesis and ATP Generation 849  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chlorophyll-Protein Complexes Can Transfer Either Excitation Energy or Electrons 850  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "A Photosystem Contains Chlorophylls in Antennae and a Reaction Center 851  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Thylakoid Membrane Contains Two Different Photosystems Working in Series 852  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Photosystem II Uses a Manganese Cluster to Withdraw Electrons from Water 853  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Cytochrome "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "b_{6}-f"
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": " Complex Connects Photosystem II to Photosystem I 854  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Photosystem I Carries Out the Second Charge-Separation Step in the Z Scheme 855  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Chloroplast ATP Synthase Uses the Proton Gradient Generated by the Photosynthetic Light Reactions to Produce ATP 855  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Proton-Motive Force for ATP Production in Mitochondria and Chloroplasts Is Essentially the Same 856  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chemiosmotic Mechanisms Evolved in Stages 856  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "By Providing an Inexhaustible Source of Reducing Power, Photosynthetic Bacteria Overcame a Major Evolutionary Obstacle 857  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Photosynthetic Electron-Transport Chains of Cyanobacteria Produced Atmospheric Oxygen and Permitted New Life-Forms 857  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 860  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "THE GENETIC SYSTEMS OF MITOCHONDRIA AND CHLOROPLASTS 861  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Genetic Systems of Mitochondria and Chloroplasts Resemble Those of Prokaryotes 861  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Over Time, Mitochondria and Chloroplasts Have Exported Most of Their Genes to the Nucleus by Gene Transfer 862  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mitochondria Have a Relaxed Codon Usage and Can Have a Variant Genetic Code 864  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chloroplasts and Bacteria Share Many Striking Similarities 865  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Organellar Genes Are Maternally Inherited in Animals and Plants 866  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Mutations in Mitochondrial DNA Can Cause Severe Inherited Diseases 866  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Why Do Mitochondria and Chloroplasts Maintain a Costly Separate System for DNA Transcription and Translation? 867  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Summary 868  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Problems 869  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "References 871  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Chapter 15 Cell Signaling 873  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "PRINCIPLES OF CELL SIGNALING 873  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Extracellular Signals Can Act Over Short or Long Distances 874  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Extracellular Signal Molecules Bind to Specific Receptors 875  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Each Cell Is Programmed to Respond to Specific Combinations of Extracellular Signals 876  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "There Are Three Major Classes of Cell-Surface Receptor Proteins 878  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Cell-Surface Receptors Relay Signals Via Intracellular Signaling Molecules 879  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Intracellular Signals Must Be Specific and Robust in a Noisy Cytoplasm 881  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Intracellular Signaling Complexes Form at Activated Cell-Surface Receptors 882  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Modular Interaction Domains Mediate Interactions Between Intracellular Signaling Proteins 883  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Relationship Between Signal and Response Varies in Different Signaling Pathways 885  "
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "The Speed of a Response Depends on the Turnover of Signaling Molecules 886"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
